<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Work song · date unknown · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Bahay Kubo</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Filipino folk song sung in schools about a small stilt house with vegetables growing all around it.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Filipino lowland communities</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tagalog</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Philippines</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Work song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahay-kubo">Bahay Kubo</a>&quot; is a famous Filipino folk song, often sung in schools, about a small house surrounded by vegetables. It takes its name from the báhay kúbo, a type of stilt house indigenous to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> and the traditional basic design of houses among almost all the lowland peoples of the islands. The Filipino term roughly means &quot;country house&quot;, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagalog-language">Tagalog</a>: báhay (&quot;house&quot;) comes from Proto-Malayo-Polynesian *balay, a public or community building, and kúbo (&quot;hut&quot;) from *kubu, a field hut in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> fields. The English name &quot;nipa hut&quot; was introduced in the American colonial era.</p><p>A bahay kubo was raised on posts of bamboo or hardwood against floods and pests, built of wood, bamboo, palm leaves and cogon grass, traditionally without nails, with a tall, steep thatched roof and large windows for air. Usually it was one room where the whole family ate, slept and worked, reached by a ladder; the space beneath held harvested crops, tools and livestock, and a separate granary, the kamálig, held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>. Under Spanish rule its design shaped the stone-based bahay na bato of the wealthier classes.</p><p>The nipa hut is an icon of Philippine culture and stands for the Filipino value of bayanihan, a spirit of communal unity and shared effort. The song gives a generalised picture of what such a house might have looked like before colonial rule: a home surrounded by locally grown plants. It does not take into account the many other native house forms, such as those of the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a>, whose architecture shows strong Islamic influence.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song about a small country house and the vegetables and plants that grow around it, a homely picture of rural life.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Tagalog</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Tagalog</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tl">Bahay kubo, kahit munti,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A nipa hut, though tiny,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tl">ang halaman doon ay sari-sari:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the plants there are of every kind:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tl">singkamas at talong, sigarilyas at mani,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>jicama and eggplant, winged bean and peanut,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tl">sitaw, bataw, patani,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>yardlong bean, hyacinth bean, lima bean,</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tl">Kundol, patola, upo't kalabasa,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Wax gourd, sponge gourd, bottle gourd and squash,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tl">At saka mayroon pang labanos, mustasa,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and besides there are still radish, mustard,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tl">sibuyas, kamatis, bawang at luya.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>onion, tomato, garlic and ginger.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tl">Sa paligid-ligid ay puno ng linga.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>All around it is full of sesame.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional; as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bahay_kubo" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (5)</summary><ul><li>line 1.1: 'bahay kubo', literally 'cube house', the Filipino stilt hut of bamboo and nipa palm.</li><li>line 1.3: singkamas is jicama (yam bean); sigarilyas the winged bean.</li><li>line 1.4: sitaw is the yardlong (string) bean; bataw the hyacinth bean (lablab); patani the lima bean.</li><li>line 2.1: kundol is the wax (winter) gourd; patola the sponge gourd (luffa); upo the bottle gourd; kalabasa squash or pumpkin.</li><li>line 2.4: linga is sesame.</li></ul></details></section>
